  "slug": "ai-erasure-and-the-social-contract-of-education",
  "title": "AI-Erasure and the Social Contract of Education",
  "excerpt": "Uncertain post-graduation employment prospects have released an unprecedented crisis of generational anxiety. The fear of unemployment in economies built for AI Agents and powered by Hyperscale AI Campuses has sparked an unexpected awakening. The realisation that the “social contract” of education is breaking down into a slow silt has unleashed an extraordinary crisis of generational […]",
  "body": [
    "Uncertain post-graduation employment prospects have released an unprecedented crisis of generational anxiety. The fear of unemployment in economies built for AI Agents and powered by Hyperscale AI Campuses has sparked an unexpected awakening. The realisation that the “social contract” of education is breaking down into a slow silt has unleashed an extraordinary crisis of generational unease. “AI anxiety” erodes students’ career adaptability, decision-making confidence, and overall life satisfaction. Universities lag in integrating AI tools and navigating academic integrity, leaving students caught between outdated campus policies and corporate demands for workplace AI fluency. The realisation that students are being trained for, and indebted to, an economic model that is simultaneously automating away entry-level jobs and skilled graduate roles has sparked restiveness and city marches. While individual city triggers vary and some grievances shadow others, the unifying driver of youth uneasiness is the realisation that the “social contract” of schooling is facing AI-erasure. These triggers range from underfunded education, higher tuition fees for useless degrees, declining test scores, dilapidated buildings, overcrowded classrooms, poor university conditions, erasure of post-graduation jobs by AI Agents, broader corruption concerns, financial exigency from budget shortfalls, cost-of-living increases, faculty shortages, chronic failures to replace absent teachers, low wages, cuts in academic appointments, college admission stress, automation of entry-level jobs, to the uncertain post-graduation employment prospects in economies reshaped by agentic AI. Eventually, simultaneous loose mobilisations in Tirana, Durrës, Korçä, Shkodër, Vlorë, Buenos Aires, Vienna, Graz, Linz, Salzburg, and Klagenfurt will combine into a political conversation. The centre cannot hold. The things that once held us together are falling apart. No still point, no epicentre anywhere. Students live their lives in widening circles that reach out across the world. They circle the university tower, the high temple of knowing. But they are doubtful whether they can complete this last one. Yet they give themselves to it, unsure whether they are a storm or a falcon. Early stages of a movement are emerging in cities that seem quiet amid a first-time super-cycle of national elections in Spain, France, Italy and Poland over the next twelve months. In Buenos Aires, students, faculty, trade unionists, and opposition supporters assembled outside parliament, demanding a law to finance universities. They strongly opposed the refusal to enact legislation to boost education funding. In Austria, the Federal Ministry proposed a 14 per cent budget cut (about €2.5 billion) for 22 public universities for the 2028–2030 budget period. The universities warned that the cuts could force massive staff and position cuts, potentially eliminating roughly 10,000 positions. In Brussels, during student protests over university registration fees, authorities used tear gas and water cannons to disperse protesters. In Spain, thousands of students, teachers, and parents launched an indefinite regional strike in Madrid to protest chronic underfunding. Key demands include smaller class sizes, increased funding, monthly wage increases of up to €600, and shorter teaching hours. Traditionally, university graduates enter the workforce in basic analytical roles, but the Spiritus Mundi of the Intelligent Era portends the death of “entry-level” graduate roles. Generative AI has erased these tasks. Students in countries with already fragile youth employment markets are restless because they face double jeopardy: high living costs today and a rapidly shrinking pool of traditional junior positions tomorrow. In France, Belgium, Germany, and Austria, the students claim that public education infrastructure is crumbling and structurally out of step with global transformations. This creates a structural mismatch between outdated curricula and the AI-driven realities of the future. Students point out that while corporate giants push AI adoption, underfunded universities fail to equip them with the advanced creative, technical, or highly adaptable skills needed to remain resilient to automated displacement. In Argentina, the cost of the AI reskilling race has sparked massive student-led resistance to severe austerity measures and budget cuts to public higher education. In an era where navigating the AI job market requires heavily subsidised tech resources, research grants, and extensive digital reskilling, slashing university funding effectively prices lower- and middle-class students out of the future workforce. This fuels intense anger over widening social inequality. Young people fear AI in the job market, but they also push back against algorithmic gatekeeping and a lack of trust in institutions. In France, scholars have directed that anger at opaque, algorithm-driven university selection and admissions processes. This has bred broader institutional distrust; students feel like cogs in a machine, with cold tech architectures dictating their academic and professional paths rather than holistic human investment in their futures. On the one hand, employers now expect entry-level workers to do what AI cannot mimic.  On the other hand, students are reconfiguring their education goals to include AI-proof majors, qualifications perceived as less vulnerable to automation, and high-empathy fields."
  ],
